WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
ladies and gentlemen  all across the globe the label made in  europe  is a symbol for the top quality of your  products  and you  europe's entrepreneurs  you can be rightly proud of this  but  made in europe means so much more than  reliable products  made in europe means that a product  comes from a place where we protect the  environment  and where workers earn a decent wage  it means that it comes from a continent  that has embarked on the modernization  of its economy and society  towards a more sustainable way of living  and working  in building this new european growth  model  industry is our indispensable ally  and i'm delighted to share some thoughts  with you  at this year's industry days  on how industry and the european  commission can complement each other  to unlock our green and digital future  just two days ago we presented a crucial  piece of this agenda  and this is europe's first ever chips  act  semiconductors have become front page  news in these months  when the global chip shortage has slowed  down the global recovery  factories closed in east asia and entire  production lines here in the european  union came to a standstill  however europe's global semiconductors  market share is only 10  today  and most of our supplies come from a  handful of producers outside europe  i think we simply cannot afford this  dependency and uncertainty any longer  and this is why by 2030  20 of the world's microchips production  should be here in europe  that's twice as much as today in a  market  that is set to double in the next decade  so it means quadrupling today's european  production  allow me for a moment to reverse our  point of view  instead of focusing on what we do not  have  let me focus on europe's strength in the  field of semiconductors  europe is the world's center  for semiconductor research  and europe is strong in some specific  areas  such as chips for cars or for industry  4.0  their crucial niche where europe is a  global leader  but i want us to become  a strong player all across the chip's  value chain  the european chips act will back this  ambition with considerable investment  and this will come on top  of more than 30 billion euros of public  investment already for seen  backed by next generation eu  our recovery program  and by horizon europe  as well as national budgets  and to put numbers in perspective  european companies in the semiconductors  field are now investing roughly 6  billion euros per year  so the european chips act can be a game  changer  our work will focus in particular on  five areas first on research  we will invest in a field where we are  already punching above our weight  focusing for instance on transistors  below three nanometers  and on disruptive technologies on  artificial intelligence  thus on research europe can go from  strength to strength  the second point is from the lab  to the fab  we must translate our excellence and  research into industrial innovation  so we will invest in ships design and in  pilot lines for prototypes  thus bridging the gap between the  laboratory  and the actual manufacturing of the most  innovative chips  my third point  is on production capacity  europe needs new advanced production  facilities  these facilities come with huge upfront  costs and therefore  public support can be necessary to  attract private investment  and for this reason we are adapting our  state aid rules  of course under a set of strict  conditions  and this will allow  for the first time  public support  for european  first-of-a-kind production facilities  which of course benefit all of europe  my fourth point is  that it is not just about mega fabs  the european ships act will also support  smaller innovative companies in  accessing finally advanced skills and  industrial partners and  equity finance  and finally  it is of course on supply chains  get me right europe will always work to  keep global markets open and connected  but we need to tackle the bottlenecks  that slow down our own growth  and we can create more balanced  interdependencies  and build supply chains that we can  trust  we will work on semiconductor  partnerships with like-minded partners  from the united states for example to  japan  and this is in the world's interest and  of course it is also in our own interest  this is of course equally true with  respect to our green transition  europe is the most ambitious continent  in the world  when it comes to the path to a net zero  future  we want to be the first climate neutral  continent by 2050  and you know that for this  we will reduce co2 emissions by at least  55 by 2030.  here also  industry is our close partner  because europe's entrepreneurs seize the  opportunities of innovative and clean  technology much earlier than others  just look at the large number of patents  in the field of climate and energy  they are striking proof of this  pioneering role  europe is there to nurture and support  this commitment in the best possible way  and let me illustrate this with three  recent examples  firstly europe helps with ideas  look at this conference  its main topics are a case in point  in the last two days you have discussed  how the construction sector can overcome  shortages of wood and other materials  for example by recycling materials or  using them more efficiently  you spoke about how european car makers  will profit  from our european battery alliance  because sustainable batteries made in  europe  are long-awaited alternative to imports  from asia  and you discussed what europe can do  to support energy intensive industry  to overcome the recent price hike  actually a topic  i will shortly come back  to in a more detail  your discussions show  that the question is not anymore whether  europe should become carbon neutral by  2050.  the question is how to get there most  efficiently  of course the european union industry  days  are an excellent opportunity to drive  this debate forward  and i call on industry to bring in its  expertise even more  when we finalize the transition pathways  together  the road map for different industrial  domains  europe also supports industries  transition  with the level of investment never  before seen on this scale and this is my  second point  just look at next generation eu our 800  billion euro recovery program  with next generation eu  we are investing in our sustainable and  digital future  like never before  and  we're tackling the structural challenges  of our economies  be it electric cars  green  hydrogen or a european renovation wave  that will make our homes and offices  more climate friendly  and our heating cheaper  450 million europeans are demanding  these changes  and i do not have to tell you  how much europe's industry can profit  from that  europe will be at your side in turbulent  times and this is my final point  of course  i'm speaking about the current rise of  gas prices  europe imports 90 percent of its gas  and demand is rising across the world  first of all because of the global  recovery  but current geopolitical tensions of  course come at a price too  we import more than 40 percent of our  gas from russia  and apparently russia has no interest in  increasing supplies right now  despite peak prices  just look at gasprom its storage in  europe is nowhere near the level it  should be  and gas flows through the jamal pipeline  and ukraine remain low  all this shows that gas problem does not  behave like a regular gas supplier  corporate interests  and politics  seem to be closely intertwined  but the good news is that europe has  done its homework  since russia's occupation and annexation  of crimea in 2014.  today we have more than 20 large lng  facilities that european ports and gas  infrastructures  are deeply interconnected  this reduces our dependency on russian  gas and allows us to diversify our  supplies  we are actively engaging now with  partners like the united states norway  algeria azerbaijan qatar  the recent energy council with the  united states and my joint declaration  with president biden  are only the most recent signs for this  unprecedented cooperation  and we can do so much more  we can jointly procure gas to become a  more significant player on the  international markets  we can better use and expand our gas  storage facilities across europe  and we can build up strategic gas  reserves  like we have in place for oil  these and other measures the commission  will shortly propose to member states  alongside these measures however  the european green deal remains our most  effective insurance  against any future increase in  electricity and energy prices  for it is the co2 heavy old energies oil  coal and gas  that are becoming more and more  expensive  and it is our dependence on the import  that makes us so vulnerable to price  hikes  on the other hand the costs  for renewables  has been constantly falling over the  past 10 years  and every kilowatt hour of electricity  that europe generates from the sun wind  hydropower and biomass  makes us more independent from russian  gas and other energy imports  this is why we have to accelerate the  development and the deployment of  renewables everywhere in  europe and we will issue additional  recommendations to member states  on how to speed up approval processes  ladies and gentlemen  the right answer to the current price  increase is not less climate protection  or less ambition  the right answer is more and more  effective climate protection  and i know that i can count on industry  in this transition on your innovative  spirit  on your support for our european project  and on your proven dedication to  strengthen europe as the home of some of  the world's most innovative companies  the european commission stands ready to  support you on our common path  for a sustainable economy made in europe  thank you very much

